      "why": "The yellow switch at R4C4 reverses two blocks far away: the red block at R6C6 (stuck head-to-head with the blue block at R6C5; reversed, it leaves and opens gate C) and the green block at R6C2 (reversed, it would face the purple block at R7C2 head-to-head for good). Its own departure also turns the red spinner beside it (R4C5) from left to up - aiming it at gate C. So the switch has a window: green must already have left upward through gate D, which opens only when its link, the purple spinner at R5C3, has left upward; that needs the column above it cleared, which needs the three top-left pieces taken in one exact order (R2C2 first: each leaving turns the next counter-clockwise spinner to face the empty side). After the switch, the red spinner must leave through gate C before the purple counter-clockwise spinner below it (R5C5) leaves - that one is free from the first move but turns the red spinner if it goes early, which a player who releases free spinners at once will do. Checked over every winning line (896): only 1 of the 6 legal first moves keeps the board solvable; the switch always fires at step 7-9, after green and the gate D link have left; the red spinner always leaves before R5C5.",
